User wants:
1. Create calc.ts with add function
2. Create calc.test.ts with three plain assertion checks
3. Run with node --experimental-strip-types
4. Refactor add into sum, keep add as wrapper
5. Rerun tests

Simple task. Working dir: __CWD__\opencode\tools-diff

Let me create files. "Plain assertion checks" — use node:assert. Test file needs to run under node --experimental-strip-types, so TypeScript only, no imports from packages.

Let me check node version maybe. --experimental-strip-types requires Node 22.6+. Let me just try.

Create calc.ts:
```ts
export function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}
```

calc.test.ts:
```ts
import assert from "node:assert";
import { add } from "./calc.ts";

assert.strictEqual(add(1, 2), 3);
assert.strictEqual(add(-1, 1), 0);
assert.strictEqual(add(0, 0), 0);
console.log("all tests passed");
```

Note: --experimental-strip-types requires explicit file extension in imports — "./calc.ts" works (actually with type stripping, importing "./calc.ts" is correct; "./calc" would fail).

Then refactor:
```ts
export function sum(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}

export function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return sum(a, b);
}
```

Rerun tests.

Caveman mode active but code normal. Keep responses terse.

